          "widget": {
            "name": "cde9927f",
            "multilineTextboxSpec": {
              "lines": [
                "### <span style=\"text-align:center;display:block;\">SHAP Feature Importance</span>\n",
                "\n",
                "<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Toronto delay risk is primarily driven by spatial factors, while NYC is more influenced by incident type. Temporal and demand variables are important in both cities, though their impact varies. This highlights that delay risk is multi-dimensional and differs structurally across cities.</span>"
              ]
            }
          },
          "position": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 7,
            "width": 12,
            "height": 2
          }
        },
        {
          "widget": {
            "name": "3da89b05",
            "multilineTextboxSpec": {
              "lines": [
                "### <span style=\"text-align:center;display:block;\">Temporal Driver - Hour</span>\n",
                "\n",
                "<span style=\"text-align:justify;display:block\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Delay risk increases during peak hours, as shown by higher SHAP values, indicating congestion-driven effects. This pattern is consistent across both cities, though the strength of impact varies, reflecting city-specific operational dynamics.</span></span>"
              ]
            }
          },
          "position": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 15,
            "width": 12,
            "height": 2
          }
        },
        {
          "widget": {
            "name": "55704b34",
            "multilineTextboxSpec": {
              "lines": [
                "### <span style=\"text-align:center;display:block\">SHAP Summary Plot (Beeswarm)</span>\n",
                "\n",
                "<span style=\"text-align:justify;display:block;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Toronto’s delay risk is predominantly driven by spatial and temporal factors, with location\\_area and hour showing the widest SHAP spread, indicating strong variability across regions and time-of-day conditions.In contrast, NYC exhibits a more incident-driven structure, where incident\\_category dominates, while demand and temporal features contribute consistently but with comparatively lower magnitude than Toronto’s spatial effects.</span></span>"
              ]
            }
          },
          "position": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 24,
